<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Arverni</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.) · capital Gergovia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Leading people of central Gaul, rulers of the Auvergne, whose noble Vercingetorix united Gaul against Caesar in 52 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Arverni held the volcanic uplands and the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limagne">Limagne</a> plain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-auvergne">Auvergne</a>. By the 2nd century BCE they dominated much of southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luernios">Luernios</a> was remembered by Greek writers for scattering gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> to his followers, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> led a vast army against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their power rested on a network of client peoples, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gabali">Gabali</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vellavi">Vellavi</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cadurci">Cadurci</a>, and on great oppida like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corent">Corent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, which imported Italian wine on a huge scale. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> kingship was abolished, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celtillus">Celtillus</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>, was put to death for seeking to restore it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bituitus">Bituitus</a> was defeated by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in 121 BCE. In 52 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in revolt and repulsed Caesar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gergovia">Gergovia</a>, but surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alesia">Alesia</a> and was later executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The Arverni were treated leniently, and their Roman capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clermont-ferrand">Augustonemetum</a> grew into Clermont-Ferrand.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/arverni.jpg" alt="Arverni" width="320" height="301"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Averni_coin_depicting_warrior_5th_to_1st_century_BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 51 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gergovia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Luernios, Bituitus, Vercingetorix</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=arverni" aria-label="Arverni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 567 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arverni lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5074 823L5304 942M5074 823L5040 846M5074 823L5080 854"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5040 846h0M5080 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5074 823h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5074 823h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gergovia. <a href="/atlas?year=-176&amp;focus=arverni">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

